<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="sep-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="sep-title">Separating you from the practice</h2>
      <p>This is the most common weakness in a doctor's records. A single account for everything hides both income and expenses.</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Use one bank account for professional receipts and costs</li>
        <li>List shared costs, such as a car or a phone used partly for work, and ask how they may be treated</li>
        <li>Record equipment purchases separately from running costs</li>
        <li>Keep invoices for conferences, journals, professional indemnity insurance and medical council registration renewals</li>
        <li>If you employ a helper, keep payment records</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Three tax questions to raise</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Is presumptive income open to you?</dt><dd>Medical is a specified profession under section 62(4) of the Income-tax Act, 2025. Section 58 lets a resident individual or firm (not an LLP) treat 50% of receipts as income, or more if actually earned, up to ₹50 lakh, or ₹75 lakh if cash receipts are within 5%. Ask whether it suits you.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Do you need to keep full accounts or face an audit?</dt><dd>Specified professions must keep books under section 62. A tax audit applies generally above ₹50 lakh of receipts, and in some cases where you declare less than the presumptive figure.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Is your TDS credit complete?</dt><dd>Hospitals may deduct tax on fees. Compare your statement with the portal before you file.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="gst-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="gst-title" class="first-h2">GST: often exempt, not always</h2>
      <p>Secondary sources explain that health care services by a clinical establishment or an authorised medical practitioner are generally exempt under GST notifications, and that doctors supplying only exempt services need not register. But "doctor" does not mean every receipt is exempt:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Selling medicines, implants or consumables from your own clinic may be treated differently.</li>
        <li>Fees for things that are not health care, such as endorsements or some rentals, may be taxable.</li>
        <li>Cosmetic or elective work needs specific advice. We could not confirm the position from the sources we read.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Ask a professional to review your full list of receipts.</p>

      <h2 id="year">A simple yearly routine</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Each month:</strong> file statements from hospitals, and note receipts and expenses.</li>
        <li><strong>Each quarter:</strong> ask about advance tax, because income is not always taxed at source.</li>
        <li><strong>Before filing:</strong> match all hospital statements with the portal's tax statements.</li>
        <li><strong>After filing:</strong> keep the working file. Income shown by hospitals should be traceable to your return.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Have you worked with visiting consultants who have several hospitals?</li>
        <li>How will you track each hospital's statement against TDS?</li>
        <li>Will you tell me about advance tax during the year?</li>
        <li>How do you deal with the salary and professional income in the same return?</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Running a clinic with staff and equipment? See {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-clinics/", "accounting for clinics" %}.</p>

